Found it. `getSession()` in `src/session/session.ts:53` calls `forgetUser()` unconditionally, before even checking whether a session is stored:

```ts
export async function getSession(): Promise<Session | null> {
  forgetUser();                              // ← wipes Amplitude's identity every time
  const raw = await AsyncStorage.getItem(STORAGE_KEY);
  ...
```

`SessionProvider` calls `getSession()` once on every app mount (`src/session/session-context.tsx:39`), so `forgetUser()` — which calls `amplitude.reset()`, rotating the device id and clearing the user id — fires on *every single launch*, even when a valid signed-in session is found right after. And when a session *is* found, `getSession()` never re-calls `identifyUser()`, so the Amplitude identity stays reset. Net effect: a returning signed-in learner looks like a brand-new anonymous device to Amplitude on every restart, exactly as described.

Worth noting: `src/session/session.spec.ts` already has a test (`signOut > "is safe to call with no session"`) that expects `forgetUser` to fire from `signOut()` itself — that test currently fails against the code as written, which is a second signal pointing at the same root cause.

**Proposed fix** — move the "forget" out of the read path and into the actual sign-out event, and re-affirm identity on a successful read:

```ts
export async function signOut(): Promise<void> {
  await AsyncStorage.removeItem(STORAGE_KEY);
  forgetUser();
}

export async function getSession(): Promise<Session | null> {
  const raw = await AsyncStorage.getItem(STORAGE_KEY);
  if (raw === null) return null;

  try {
    const session = JSON.parse(raw) as Session;
    identifyUser(session.accountId);
    return session;
  } catch {
    await AsyncStorage.removeItem(STORAGE_KEY);
    return null;
  }
}
```

I traced this through every existing test in `session.spec.ts` and it makes all of them pass, including the currently-failing one. Per this project's process, I'd write this up as a PR (plan first, then the diff, reviewed by someone else) rather than push straight to main — want me to proceed on that basis?

